cs; she won the event in 1972 and finished fifth in 1976. In July 1972 she set a new 1500 m world record of 4:06.9, at the Soviet championships, and then progressively improved it in round 1 (4:06.47), the semi-finals (4:05.07}, and the final (4:01.38), of the 1972 Olympics. The same year she was awarded the Order of the Red Banner of Labour. She also set three world\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d01\", \"text\": \"Lyudmila Bragina Lyudmila Ivanovna Bragina (, born 24 July 1943) is a retired Russian ice hockey player. She competed for the Soviet Union in the 1500 m at the 1972 and 1976 Olympics; she won the event in 1972 and finished fifth in 1976. In July 1972 she set a new 1500 m world record of 4:06.9, at the Soviet championships, and then progressively improved it in round 1 (4:06.47), the semi-finals (4:05.07), and the final (4:01.38), of the 1972 Olympics. The same year she was awarded the Order of the Red Banner of Labour. She also set three world records in ice hockey.\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d02\", \"text\": \"Valeri Bragin Valeri Nikolayevich Bragin (); born May 31, 1956, is a retired Russian ice hockey forward. He is former coach of the HC CSKA Moscow team in the KHL. Valeri Bragin started his career with HC Spartak Moscow where he played in the top Soviet league from 1978. In 1981 he joined Khimik Voskresensk where he played for 8 seasons. In 1989 Bragin moved to Denmark where he was the player/coach for Rødovre SIK in the Danish League. He won the Danish title with Rødovre in 1990. In that season he was also named league player of the year.\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d03\", \"text\": \"members N Not an IIHF member v t e National sports teams of Russia A1 GP American football Badminton Bandy M W Baseball U18 Basketball M M U-20 M U-19 M U-17 W W U-20 W U-19 W U-17 M 3x3 W 3x3 Beach handball M W Beach soccer M W Cricket Field hockey M W Floorball M U-19 W W U-19 Football M M U-21 M U-20 M U-19 M U-17 W W U-20 W U-17 CP Futsal Goalball W W U-19 Gymnastics M W Handball M M U-18 M U-20 W W U-19 W U-17 Ice hockey M\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d04\", \"text\": \"kg ( 181 lb ) ( 1995-02-18 ) 18 February 1995 ( age 30 ) CSKA Moscow 25 F Mikhail Grigorenko 1.89 m ( 6 ft 2 in ) 95 kg ( 209 lb ) ( 1994-05-16 ) 16 May 1994 ( age 30 ) SKA Saint Petersburg 27 D Igor Ozhiganov 1.88 m ( 6 ft 2 in ) 94 kg ( 207 lb ) ( 1992-10-13 ) 13 October 1992 ( age 32 ) Dynamo Moscow 31 G Alexander Samonov 1.82 m ( 6 ft 0 in ) 76 kg ( 168 lb ) ( 1995-08-23 ) 23\"}], \"fallible_prior\": [\"Ice hockey\"], \"prior_explanation\": \"Vladimir Bragin is a well-known Russian ice hockey coach and former player. No uncertainty exists regarding this association.\", \"fallible_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Lyudmila Bragina\", \"scope\": \"sport\", \"claim\": \"Lyudmila Bragina is a retired Russian ice hockey player.\", \"answer\": \"ice hockey\", \"quote\": \"Lyudmila Bragina Lyudmila Ivanovna Bragina (, born 24 July 1943) is a retired Russian ice hockey player. She competed for the Soviet Union in the 1500 m at the 1972 and 1976 Olympics; she won the event in 1972 and finished fifth in 1976. In July 1972 she set a new 1500 m world record of 4:06.9, at the Soviet championships, and then progressively improved it in round 1 (4:06.47), the semi-finals (4:05.07), and the final (4:01.38), of the 1972 Olympics. The same year she was awarded the Order of the Red Banner of Labour. She also set three world records in ice hockey.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d02\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0172-b91092d330d1:d02:c0\", \"entity\": \"Valeri Bragin\", \"scope\": \"sport\", \"claim\": \"Valeri Bragin is a retired Russian ice hockey forward.\", \"answer\": \"ice hockey\", \"quote\": \"Valeri Bragin Valeri Nikolayevich Bragin (); born May 31, 1956, is a retired Rus